    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper is part of an on-going effort to improve representation of fault zones in fluid flow models based on the analysis of an analogue sandstone reservoir in the Valley of Fire State Park. Generally, most fault zone permeability studies assume slip surfaces as continuous features that enhance fault-parallel flow due to the parallel plate representation. However, our field observations and laboratory analyses suggest that slip surfaces are highly heterogeneous features that are subjected to diagenetic and cataclastic alteration. As a result, petrophysical properties of slip surfaces such as porosity, permeability and connectivity do not remain constant throughout the fault evolution. We analyze geometric, temporal, textural and mineralogical aspects of slip surfaces and fault rock to better represent fault zones in flow models.\r\n\r\nResults of this study are subsequently used in permeability upscaling of fault zone blocks and compared with previous results of flow modeling through fault zones.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
